WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:05.820
In this video I’ll be talking about a remarkable
alternative theory or interpretation of Quantum

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:08.019
mechanics; Pilot wave theory.

00:00:08.019 --> 00:00:13.920
aka de Broglie Bohm theory, aka Bohmian mechanics.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.800
There are people who think that this theory
has been disproved or discredited, but more

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:23.199
recently, there are people who think that
Bohmian mechanics explains everything quantum

00:00:23.199 --> 00:00:27.099
mechanics does without any quantum weirdness.

00:00:27.099 --> 00:00:31.919
Both these positions are wrong, so I thought
it was time to make a new video clarifying

00:00:31.919 --> 00:00:36.710
all this, since my old videos on this are
3 years old now.

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:38.789
So what is Bohmian mechanics?

00:00:38.789 --> 00:00:42.370
To answer that, I better quickly explain what
Quantum mechanics is.

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:48.199
100 or more years ago, physicists were finally
able to do experiments on really small things

00:00:48.199 --> 00:00:53.420
like atoms, and though that was cool, they
weren’t really expecting to be surprised.

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:58.390
Newton’s laws and the rest of physics as
they knew it should apply just as well to

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:00.829
galaxies as electrons.

00:01:00.829 --> 00:01:02.590
But that’s not what they found.

00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:05.229
Take for example, the double slit experiment.

00:01:05.229 --> 00:01:09.939
When you shoot things one at a time toward
two doors, you expect that if it makes it

00:01:09.939 --> 00:01:15.509
throw, it will go through one of those doors
and end up somewhere behind it, so that you

00:01:15.509 --> 00:01:18.020
eventually get two clumps.

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:22.630
They decided to try this simple experiment
with really small things like atoms.

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:27.000
You shot them out one at a time but you can’t
actually see them going in their path and

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:31.119
so you can’t see which door they went through,
but you’d expect them to do the same thing

00:01:31.119 --> 00:01:35.520
essentially as before and end up in the same
two clumps.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:36.960
Only they didn’t.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.270
They ended up in these weird patterns like
this.

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:41.070
Physicists scratched their heads.

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:46.229
Then they tried the same thing again, but
this time they closed one of the doors randomly.

00:01:46.229 --> 00:01:51.259
Now the particle had to be going through just
one door, so it had to just land somewhere

00:01:51.259 --> 00:01:52.680
behind that door.

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:57.420
And they were right; the final pattern was
just what they expected in the first place.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:03.619
But then they tried it again with both doors
open at once and... it was the same strange

00:02:03.619 --> 00:02:04.619
pattern.

00:02:04.619 --> 00:02:09.440
So they scratched their collective heads again-
and came up with an idea!

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:12.580
They proposed Newton was wrong after all.

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:17.250
Actually, what really happens is, when an
object has more than one option available

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:23.500
to it, and no one is checking which is does,
it kind of does all of them at once.

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:28.580
This is called a quantum superposition- they
gave it this fancy name because they didn’t

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:33.610
really know what they meant by doing all possible
things at once.

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:35.970
This sounds totally crazy, but ‘look!’

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:36.970
they said.

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:38.370
It can explain the double slit experiment.

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:43.980
See, the atom doesn’t just go through one
door or the other, it goes through both.

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:47.230
Then, it’s able to interfere with itself!

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:50.080
And that gives the strange pattern.

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:54.921
When there’s only one door open, it isn’t
in a superposition, so it can’t interfere

00:02:54.921 --> 00:03:01.140
with itself and it just does the usual thing-
and so you get back the normal pattern.

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:06.590
This quantum superposition thing got applied
to many many many more experiments, and it

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:08.840
always made the right prediction.

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:15.090
And so, even though Quantum mechanics is crazy,
we’re all forced to accept it by experimental

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:16.090
facts.

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:18.200
At least, that’s the convention wisdom.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:20.290
Then came Bohmian mechanics.

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:26.950
The aim of this theory is to explain all the
same experiments as quantum mechanics can,

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:29.060
but without superposition.

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:34.390
In fact, you can prove that Bohmian mechanics
produces exactly the same results as quantum

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:39.870
mechanics for all possible experiments, but
the objects in this theory are always in one

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:42.370
place, and have one speed.

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:43.380
How is this possible?

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:47.460
Let’s look at the double slit experiment
to understand.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:52.570
In Bohmian mechanics, the particle must have
a path from it’s start, through one of the

00:03:52.570 --> 00:03:58.230
slits to the back wall since the particle
is in just one spot at each time.

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:03.050
In contrast, in quantum mechanics, the particle
takes every possible path.

00:04:03.050 --> 00:04:08.810
The first thing the theory needs to explain
is, if your particles all start in one place

00:04:08.810 --> 00:04:13.570
and they follow a predetermined path, why
don’t they all end up in exactly the same

00:04:13.570 --> 00:04:15.620
spot.

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:20.000
Bohmian mechanics explains this by saying,
you don’t actually know exactly where the

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:21.870
particles started out.

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:27.639
This first particle might have been just left
of that one, which meant they end up going

00:04:27.639 --> 00:04:28.909
to different places.

00:04:28.909 --> 00:04:33.080
It’s just because you don’t know exactly
where it started that you don’t know where

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:34.460
it will end up.

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:38.990
This is the exact same as in the non-quantum
case.

00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:41.370
So that sounds reasonable.

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:47.360
But a fancy way to put this is that the initial
position is a ‘hidden variable’- since

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:52.449
it’s a variable you don’t know, it’s
hidden, making Bohmian mechanics a hidden

00:04:52.449 --> 00:04:53.819
variable theory.

00:04:53.819 --> 00:04:59.689
You’ll hear the term hidden variable uses
disparagingly all the time by people so I

00:04:59.689 --> 00:05:05.099
thought I’d introduce it to you so you see
it’s not necessarily a bad thing for a theory

00:05:05.099 --> 00:05:06.960
to have hidden.

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:08.370
Anyway.

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:14.050
This is the important part; so to check you’re
paying attention, let me ask you a question.

00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:19.849
Suppose that in the situation that one door
was closed, this is the path that this particle

00:05:19.849 --> 00:05:22.800
would take according to Bohmian mechanics.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:27.300
If I opened the other door, will that path
change or not?

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:32.889
Seriously, pause the video now and think about
the answer.

00:05:32.889 --> 00:05:41.110
Well let’s see what’d happen if the path
didn’t change.

00:05:41.110 --> 00:05:46.569
Then all objects that went through this door
would end up here, and similarly for objects

00:05:46.569 --> 00:05:48.639
going through the other door.

00:05:48.639 --> 00:05:51.410
You end up with the wrong prediction.

00:05:51.410 --> 00:05:54.740
But I told you Bohmian mechanics always gets
it right.

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:59.919
So what really happens is, the particle does
change path when the other door is open- even

00:05:59.919 --> 00:06:02.039
though it’s not going through it.

00:06:02.039 --> 00:06:08.509
So if that door is open, it feels a force
pushing it into some weird wonky path like

00:06:08.509 --> 00:06:09.550
this.

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:14.569
In fact, here’s a simulation of a bunch
of paths that particles starting in slightly

00:06:14.569 --> 00:06:16.370
different positions take.

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:23.250
And you can see that where they end up really
does exactly reproduce the interference pattern.

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:28.199
So here is the key take away point from that:
there still is quantum weirdness in Bohmian

00:06:28.199 --> 00:06:30.840
mechanics, it’s just a bit hidden.

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:36.449
In standard quantum mechanics, a particle
is in a superposition of all possible places,

00:06:36.449 --> 00:06:42.090
but in Bohmian mechanics the particle is in
one place, but it’s aware of and affected

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:46.110
by all the other possible places it could
be.

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:52.800
Just to be clear; This is not a typical feature
of a classical theory of physics.

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:58.300
Bohmian mechanics has one really awesome advantage
over standard quantum mechanics; it doesn’t

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:00.289
have the measurement problem.

00:07:00.289 --> 00:07:05.610
The measurement problem is arguably the biggest
conceptual difficulty of quantum mechanics.

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:07.210
Let me explain that.

00:07:07.210 --> 00:07:12.120
In the double slit experiment, imagine if
you put little detectors on the doors that

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:15.230
flash every time a particle goes through it.

00:07:15.230 --> 00:07:18.600
Then you could find out which door the particle
went through!

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:23.949
But then you look at the pattern and you see
that it’s not the interference one.

00:07:23.949 --> 00:07:28.669
This is explained in ordinary quantum mechanics
by saying that, when you measure a particle

00:07:28.669 --> 00:07:33.969
that’s in a superposition, the superposition
collapses to just doing one thing.

00:07:33.969 --> 00:07:35.710
But why?

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:38.550
Why do quantum particles get shy like that?

00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:42.800
Bohm proposed a solution to this inside of
Bohmian mechanics called decoherence.

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:48.349
It’s basically the idea that when you measure
something, what you’re really doing is getting

00:07:48.349 --> 00:07:54.629
your measurement device to interact with it,
and that interaction makes it look like there’s

00:07:54.629 --> 00:07:55.629
this collapse.

00:07:55.629 --> 00:08:01.240
I won’t go into this much here because decoherence
deserves it’s own video- not just on how

00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:05.729
it relates to Bohmian mechanics, but also
how it relates to other interpretations like

00:08:05.729 --> 00:08:07.699
many worlds.

00:08:07.699 --> 00:08:11.539
But now let’s look at a common criticism
of Bohmian mechanics.

00:08:11.539 --> 00:08:17.439
You will hear people harp on about how Bell’s
theorem proves that no hidden variable theory

00:08:17.439 --> 00:08:20.830
can give the same results as quantum mechanics.

00:08:20.830 --> 00:08:26.699
This is clearly rubbish because Bohmian mechanics
exists and predicts everything quantum mechanics

00:08:26.699 --> 00:08:30.440
does and is a hidden variable theory.

00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:34.660
You know who also used to get frustrated by
people misinterpreting Bell’s theorem?

00:08:34.660 --> 00:08:35.850
John Bell.

00:08:35.850 --> 00:08:41.290
He was a huge fan of Bohmian mechanics and
wrote lots about it, for example this quote

00:08:41.290 --> 00:08:44.970
about the Bohm’s explanation of the double
slit experiment.

00:08:44.970 --> 00:08:47.900
He didn’t misunderstand his own theorem.

00:08:47.900 --> 00:08:53.451
What Bell’s theorem actually says is any
theory that reproduces the results of quantum

00:08:53.451 --> 00:09:02.080
mechanics, so for example, quantum mechanics
itself and Bohmian mechanics, has to be nonlocal.

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:07.700
Something is nonlocal when you have one thing
over here affect another thing over there

00:09:07.700 --> 00:09:11.370
instantly.

00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:17.130
Quantum mechanics and Bohmian mechanics are
nonlocal and the situations they’re nonlocal

00:09:17.130 --> 00:09:23.020
in are the same, basically EPR experiment
like set ups, where there’s a person on

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:27.770
each of two different sides of the universe,
and what one of them does to their particle

00:09:27.770 --> 00:09:29.980
instantly effects the others particle -because
their is entanglement in Bohmian mechanics,

00:09:29.980 --> 00:09:30.980
it’s have a difference interpretation.

00:09:30.980 --> 00:09:31.980
but it’s still there and certainly not classical.

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:33.610
This is a point I think people have gotten
confused about.

00:09:33.610 --> 00:09:37.760
You might have seen Veritasium’s beautiful
video about how you can reproduce the effect

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:43.210
of pilot wave theory by using a water phenomenon
called walking droplets.

00:09:43.210 --> 00:09:47.610
If you haven’t watched that, then do, it’s
mesmerising.

00:09:47.610 --> 00:09:53.440
These walking droplets are completely classical;
in fact in the video Derek makes them at home,

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:58.820
so you might be tempted to conclude that pilot
wave theory is totally classical since it

00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:00.680
can be mimicked classically.

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:05.110
But can all of Pilot wave be mimicked by these
water experiments?

00:10:05.110 --> 00:10:09.890
Actually, it’s only experiments where there
is isn’t entangled particles that can be

00:10:09.890 --> 00:10:14.880
mimicked this way, because when there are
entangled particles in Pilot wave theory they

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:19.440
affect each others movements no matter how
far they are from each other.

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:25.070
But classical systems can’t have these nonlocal
effects, so they can’t produce the right

00:10:25.070 --> 00:10:26.070
results.

00:10:26.070 --> 00:10:30.770
That’s why these water experiments can reproduce
all kinds of quantum phenomena like the double

00:10:30.770 --> 00:10:36.140
slit experiment, or tunneling, or harmonic
potentials, but it could never reproduce a

00:10:36.140 --> 00:10:37.420
Bell experiment.

00:10:37.420 --> 00:10:42.710
It’s impressive how much Bohmian mechanics
can explain classically, but it still has

00:10:42.710 --> 00:10:47.130
very nonclassical parts, and that’s important
to remember.

00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:53.010
To wrap up, there are plenty of big pros but
also a fair number of cons for anyone considering

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:57.910
taking Bohmian mechanics as their interpretation
of quantum mechanics.

00:10:57.910 --> 00:10:59.410
But enough being impartial.

00:10:59.410 --> 00:11:02.610
You might be wondering, do I believe in Bohmian
mechanics.

00:11:02.610 --> 00:11:09.630
No, I don’t, but I don’t believe any interpretations
since there a several that are consistent

00:11:09.630 --> 00:11:11.800
with the facts at the moment.

00:11:11.800 --> 00:11:14.130
But do I like Bohmian mechanics?

00:11:14.130 --> 00:11:15.510
Yes and no.

00:11:15.510 --> 00:11:17.810
No, because of that relativity criticism,
and also because I worry about how natural

00:11:17.810 --> 00:11:20.580
the maths of this theory is.

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:26.140
But Yes, because on some levels it’s much
more intuitive, and it answers the measurement

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:27.690
problem.

00:11:27.690 --> 00:11:32.270
But putting aside how plausible I find the
theory itself, the thing I love the most about

00:11:32.270 --> 00:11:37.800
Bohmian mechanics, aka Pilot wave theory,
is that it shows us there’s still plenty

00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:42.350
of room for debate on what quantum mechanics
actually means.

00:11:42.350 --> 00:11:47.890
That even something as seemingly fundament
as quantum superposition can be questioned.

00:11:47.890 --> 00:11:53.680
We need to keep critically investigating the
interpretation of quantum mechanics.

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:56.550
As John Bell put it “Bohm has shown us the
way.”

